      "instance_name": "marijuana_use",
      "label": "Marijuana use",
      "description": "The phrase 'don't smoke them' in the context of 'rocks' is a reference to smoking marijuana, which is a drug that is illegal or regulated in many jurisdictions. The meme uses this reference to create a humorous contrast between a harmless activity and a potentially illegal or controversial one. This requires knowledge of drug laws and social attitudes toward marijuana.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_e9728045"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_5986b726",
      "@type": "BackgroundKnowledge",
      "instance_name": "pun_or_wordplay",
      "label": "Pun or wordplay",
      "description": "The meme relies on a pun or wordplay on the word 'rocks'. The same word can refer to physical stones (for skipping) or to marijuana (for smoking). This linguistic device is central to the humor and requires background knowledge of how language can be used for comedic effect through ambiguity.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_e9728045"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_ee845d46",
      "@type": "EmotionExpression",
      "instance_name": "sarcastic_concern",
      "label": "sarcastic concern emotion",
      "description": "The emotion expressed is a sarcastic or exasperated concern, conveyed through the overlaid text 'for the love of god' and 'skip rocks, don't smoke them'. This humorously expresses disbelief or frustration at someone's misunderstanding or misinterpretation of the word 'rocks', implying the person is doing something absurd or inappropriate (smoking marijuana) instead of the innocent activity (skipping rocks). The emotion is not genuine concern but a comedic exaggeration of annoyance.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_e9728045"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_1063ac9a",
      "@type": "AnalogicalMapping",
      "instance_name": "mapping_001",
      "label": "The person is mapped as someone who is misinterpreting 'rocks' as marijuana",
      "description": "The text 'skip rocks, don't smoke them' implies that the person in the image is the one who is misinterpreting the word 'rocks' — likely thinking of marijuana instead of stones. The meme humorously projects the idea that the person is the one who is doing the 'wrong' thing (smoking rocks) instead of the 'right' thing (skipping rocks). The visual of the person skipping rocks is thus mapped onto the textual concept of someone who is confused or mistaken about what 'rocks' means, creating an analogical mapping where the person is the subject of the textual criticism.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
        "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#analysis_e9728045"
      },
      "extractionMethod": "llm_extraction_huggingface"
    },
    {
      "@id": "http://example.org/multimodal-taxonomy#dimension_1dcfac47",
      "@type": "SemioticProjection",
      "instance_name": "semiotic_projection_1",
      "label": "User projected onto the person skipping rocks",
      "description": "The user is implicitly projected onto the person in the image, who is the subject of the meme's humorous critique. The text 'for the love of god skip rocks, don't smoke them' is directed at the person's action, implying the user (the viewer) is identifying with or imagining themselves as the person who might be misinterpreting 'rocks' as marijuana. The visual of the person skipping rocks is the focal point of the meme's commentary, and the user is projected onto this visual entity as the target of the joke.",
      "belongsToAnalysis": {
